    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I'm working with a client who's been stuck on their back squat for about 4 weeks now. They aren't feeling any pain, they're eating enough, but the weight just won't move up. Can you explain how deloading or changing up the volume might help them break through this plateau from a physiological standpoint?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When you deload or reduce volume on a squat, you leave your neuromuscular system and connective tissues in a state of recovery, allowing stress tolerance to rise before the next heavy load. This prevents unnecessary fatigue from interfering with the neural adaptations responsible for generating more force. Consequently, returning to training with higher intensity often yields immediate performance improvements as your body can once again handle the required anatomical and physiological stress.",
      "source": "target",
